WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.360
Okay, I've got a few minutes to do some technical stuff on MCP that is like optional.

00:06.160 --> 00:11.279
Many of you may notice already. Some of you may not know and may not care because it's not

00:11.279 --> 00:16.800
necessary to get through the using it. But for those that do care, the next couple of minutes are

00:16.800 --> 00:23.280
for you, some technicalities about MCP. The terminology isn't that important for using it in

00:23.280 --> 00:27.399
Cloud Code. We're not going to build MCP. We're going to just use it.

00:27.399 --> 00:29.160
But you might as well know these are terms you've

00:29.160 --> 00:30.239
probably already heard.

00:30.239 --> 00:33.079
There are these three things that people talk about with MCP.

00:33.079 --> 00:36.359
The MCP host, the MCP client, the MCP server.

00:36.359 --> 00:40.520
The MCP host is the name of the overall AI application,

00:40.520 --> 00:43.200
which is running, which needs to call tools.

00:43.200 --> 00:45.920
And so, Cloud Code is an example of an MCP host.

00:45.920 --> 00:47.960
Chat GPT is an MCP host.

00:47.960 --> 00:49.039
If you're writing your own

00:49.039 --> 00:55.679
agentic app like we do in my agentic track of my AI engineer course, we use open AI agent

00:55.679 --> 01:03.520
sstk and that as an application, the software is an mcp host. That's the mcp host. The mcp client

01:03.520 --> 01:11.040
is a bit of code that runs within the mcp host where there's one of them for each of the sets of tools that you're using.

01:11.040 --> 01:15.640
And that is, again, that is something that's part baked into a Cloud Code or OpenAI

01:15.640 --> 01:19.480
Agencies DK or Chat GPT and so on.

01:19.480 --> 01:25.239
And then finally, the thing that you actually really care about is called the MCP server.

01:25.239 --> 01:33.760
And that is the thing that manages the set of tools written by somebody else. So what they give you is an MCP server,

01:33.760 --> 01:36.159
an MCP server for browsing the internet,

01:36.159 --> 01:38.879
an MCP server for looking up stock prices.

01:38.879 --> 01:40.680
These are each MCP servers,

01:40.680 --> 01:42.280
and they give you a little description of it,

01:42.280 --> 01:43.319
and that's what you take,

01:43.319 --> 01:46.400
and you give it to your MCP host, like Claude Code,

01:46.400 --> 01:48.599
and it takes that description of an MCP server,

01:48.599 --> 01:50.000
and it knows what to do.

01:50.000 --> 01:51.719
It knows how to set up a client,

01:51.719 --> 01:53.280
it knows how to launch that server,

01:53.280 --> 01:54.680
it knows how to run it.

01:54.680 --> 01:57.319
That's what's going on when you use MCP.

01:57.319 --> 01:59.840
And then the other technicality is that they can run

01:59.840 --> 02:03.980
in two different modes, perhaps two different techniques.

02:03.980 --> 02:07.120
It's known as the different transports.

02:07.120 --> 02:09.039
It can run locally, in which case,

02:09.039 --> 02:11.159
it's running on your computer.

02:11.159 --> 02:14.199
The MCP server, this thing which is gonna look up

02:14.199 --> 02:16.639
market data or whatever, is running on your

02:16.639 --> 02:21.300
computer, or it can run remotely, which means it's running up on the cloud.

02:21.300 --> 02:25.639
So if it's a JIRA-related one, maybe Atlassian is running it.

02:25.639 --> 02:30.120
If it's GitHub, we'll use GitHub's, maybe Microsoft or GitHub are running it on their

02:30.120 --> 02:33.039
servers and you're connecting remotely to it.

02:33.039 --> 02:35.120
And there are even in there that sort of subdivides.

02:35.120 --> 02:40.599
There are two different techniques. One is called SSE and is now a legacy has been replaced,

02:40.599 --> 02:42.139
but still works.

02:42.139 --> 02:44.360
And one of them is streamable HTTP,

02:44.360 --> 02:45.539
which is the new way.

02:45.539 --> 02:48.680
SSE is deprecated, but you can still use it.

02:48.680 --> 02:53.219
And the thing is that there is a big sort of confusion

02:53.219 --> 02:56.080
about this that I want to try and nicks right now.

02:56.080 --> 02:59.400
So it's a huge sort of confusion that some people have

02:59.400 --> 03:01.439
that when I say that this is these are

03:01.439 --> 03:07.360
local or remote MCP servers, I'm referring as I say to where the MCP servers actually run on your

03:07.360 --> 03:13.199
computer or or running remotely. That's that's where the thing that that figures out what tools

03:13.199 --> 03:18.080
you've got access to and what you can do with those tools. But often the implementation of those

03:18.080 --> 03:22.719
tools involves making a web call. So just because the tools are running on your computer

03:22.719 --> 03:25.400
doesn't mean you're not accessing online services

03:25.400 --> 03:26.199
in some way.

03:26.199 --> 03:27.919
For example, the market data one

03:27.919 --> 03:30.039
that we're going to try in a second that looks up stock

03:30.039 --> 03:30.759
prices.

03:30.759 --> 03:33.479
It's a local tool that runs, the MCP server

03:33.479 --> 03:34.520
runs on my computer.

03:34.520 --> 03:37.039
But for sure, it's looking up stock prices on the internet.

03:37.039 --> 03:38.800
The stock prices aren't on my computer.

03:38.800 --> 03:40.280
So just because it runs locally

03:40.280 --> 03:43.560
doesn't mean it's not connecting remotely in some way

03:43.560 --> 03:45.000
as part of its implementation.

03:45.000 --> 03:47.000
So that's one source of confusion.

03:47.000 --> 03:52.000
And the other confusion is that people sometimes think that because it's a local MCP server,

03:52.000 --> 03:55.000
and by the way, most MCP servers are local MCP servers,

03:55.000 --> 03:59.000
they think in some way that that's not real MCP, that you're not sharing.

03:59.000 --> 04:01.000
It wasn't written by somebody else.

04:01.000 --> 04:03.000
But that's all a confusion.

04:03.000 --> 04:07.039
A local, it's still completely,

04:07.039 --> 04:09.479
all the benefits of MCP applies.

04:09.479 --> 04:11.199
That code that's running locally

04:11.199 --> 04:14.319
will still have been fetched from a remote repository.

04:14.319 --> 04:17.959
It's been installed probably either with something like PIP

04:17.959 --> 04:20.839
or with UV, a modern version,

04:20.839 --> 04:23.800
or with something like MPX,

04:23.800 --> 04:25.939
if it's a node, a JavaScript program.

04:25.939 --> 04:27.959
It's been installed locally in your computer,

04:27.959 --> 04:28.920
but from a shared place, and's been installed locally in your computer, but from a shared

04:28.920 --> 04:34.879
place and then it's running on your computer. So it's just about where it actually runs,

04:34.879 --> 04:39.639
but it's still shared code, a shared tool written by somebody else. It's just about

04:39.639 --> 04:45.240
whether you're accessing it on the cloud or accessing it running on your computer. So

04:45.240 --> 04:51.639
as I say, not super important, but I do like to bring clarity to this. And just so that you understand,

04:51.639 --> 04:54.519
most MCP servers actually are local,

04:54.519 --> 04:56.800
but you've still got all the benefits of MCP

04:56.800 --> 04:59.480
and it still might well be connecting online.

04:59.480 --> 05:02.680
And that's the difference between MCP host, MCP client,

05:02.680 --> 05:04.240
and MCP server.

05:04.240 --> 05:05.899
And often when you hear someone saying,

05:05.899 --> 05:09.300
I'm using this MCP, that's shorthand for saying,

05:09.300 --> 05:11.500
I'm using this MCP server.

05:11.500 --> 05:13.779
So at this point, we're gonna go and experiment

05:13.779 --> 05:16.740
with MCP servers before we move on to skills.

05:16.740 --> 05:18.579
One of the things that's kind of odd,

05:18.579 --> 05:20.980
that's sort of funky about MCP,

05:20.980 --> 05:25.120
is that it's not a super standard way

05:25.120 --> 05:28.500
to discover MCP servers, other people have written.

05:28.500 --> 05:30.980
It's given you this great way to connect together,

05:30.980 --> 05:39.600
but it's not like there's been a kind of sort of everyone converging on one place to find MCP servers.

05:39.600 --> 05:42.680
There are a bunch of different marketplace sites,

05:42.680 --> 05:45.660
a lot of them, and there are thousands, tens of thousands,

05:45.660 --> 05:49.639
lots of MCP servers available, including very popular ones.

05:49.639 --> 05:51.639
But it's a little bit of a Wild West

05:51.639 --> 05:53.199
to understand how to navigate them.

